Sources for `ticket-runner` skill (see plans/skill-audits/ticket-runner.md for full audit):

- Original void-harness composition skill. No single external source: it is the
  canonical "execute one ticket end-to-end" conductor that the harness lacked.
- harness:backlog-autopilot — had an INLINE per-ticket cycle (plan -> TDD ->
  static UX/UI pass -> ...). That cycle is extracted here so there is one
  definition; backlog-autopilot now delegates per ticket. Removes responsibility
  overlap.
- Composes (does not reimplement): harness:tdd, harness:testing,
  harness:hexagonal-architecture, harness:domain-driven-design,
  harness:frontend-design, harness:accessibility-first, harness:security-guidance,
  harness:code-review, harness:verification-before-completion,
  harness:commit-discipline; agents type-design-analyzer, doctrine-critic,
  silent-failure-hunter; gstack /design-review, /qa, /cso; the `impeccable`
  frontend skill when installed.

Strategy: author. The load-bearing idea is the predicate-keyed triage: speed
comes from skipping passes whose trigger is false, never from skipping an
ALWAYS pass. This is the "an expert team would cover all of it" guarantee made
operational, and the anti-loophole against "go fast" pressure.
